+## Stage 4 — qualified Kbase releases + guest preflight (implemented)
+
+Stage 4 turns the Kbase plumbing into an installable/testable driver flow instead of requiring every
+user to hand-build and hand-label an ICD.
+
+### Qualified release discovery
+
+The existing release browser now also checks
+`zenithblue-oss/panvk-kbase-android` when the user explicitly taps refresh. Only Linux/glibc
+`EMULATOR` assets whose immutable tag maps to a profile in `MaliKbaseProfiles` are offered.
+Android/bionic packages and unknown profiles are deliberately excluded.
+
+The first qualified profile is `g615-v11-csf`:
+
+- Mali-G615 / Pan architecture v11
+- CSF frontend
+- Kbase UAPI 1.21
+- product ID `0xb8a3`
+- public reference device: Poco X6 Pro / Dimensity 8300-Ultra
+
+A downloaded asset still passes GitHub release SHA-256 verification and the normal AArch64/glibc
+importer. After that, DroidDeck attaches the trusted profile contract derived from the exact release
+tag. This is intentionally an allow-list: a future G720/G610/etc. release is not offered until its
+frontend/UAPI/product-id contract is added.
+
+### Package schema 4
+
+`tools/mali/package_kbase_driver.py` now emits `abi=linux-aarch64-glibc`, optional profile/provenance
+fields, and a `filesSha256` map for the ICD plus helper libraries. The importer validates those hashes
+when present and rejects mixed-architecture `.so` payloads before installation. Schema 3 remains
+readable for existing Stage-3 packages.
+
+Example:
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/mali/package_kbase_driver.py \
+  --lib dist/libvulkan_panfrost.so \
+  --out panvk-kbase-g615.zip \
+  --name "PanVK Kbase G615" \
+  --version "0.1.0-beta.3" \
+  --profile g615-v11-csf --pan-arch 11 \
+  --frontend csf --uapi 1.21 --product-id 0xb8a3 \
+  --source-repo zenithblue-oss/panvk-kbase-android \
+  --source-tag g615-v11-csf-v0.1.0-beta.3 \
+  --qualification beta \
+  --tested-device "Poco X6 Pro / Dimensity 8300-Ultra"
+```
+
+### Guest preflight
+
+Before gamescope takes over the screen, an imported ICD now gets two extra failure gates:
+
+1. `ldd` is used (when available) to report missing guest helper libraries immediately.
+2. On `MALI_KBASE`, `/dev/mali0` must actually be visible inside the guest; when `vulkaninfo` exists,
+   `vulkaninfo --summary` is run with a short timeout to exercise device enumeration before gamescope.
+
+Those checks convert several former black-screen cases into a specific session-log failure.
+
 ## What Stage 3 does **not** claim
 
 The launcher/runtime route is implemented, but a usable game stack still depends on having a
